flboot -- the FreeLFS UEFI boot loader
=====================================

flboot lives on the EFI system partition (ESP), which FreeLFS mounts at
/boot, as EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI (BOOTAA64.EFI on aarch64): the file UEFI
firmware starts by default, so no boot variables are needed. `flboot-install`
puts it there; flpkg runs that again whenever the flboot package changes.

The menu lists:
  - every kernel on the ESP (/boot/vmlinuz-*), newest first; the LTS kernel (package linux-lts,
    vmlinuz-VERSION-freelfs-lts) comes after the others as "FreeLFS (LTS kernel VERSION)", the
    fallback when a new kernel does not work with some hardware;
  - the entries in flboot/flboot.conf;
  - "Start an earlier state" (only when there are snapshots): a second screen
    with the btrfs snapshots listed in flboot/snapshots, which flsnap writes
    after every change (src/flsnap; flpkg takes one before each transaction).
    Choosing one shows a notice (read-only, nothing is kept, how to keep it),
    then starts the newest kernel with rootflags=subvol=@snapshots/N.
Up/Down (or j/k) choose, Enter starts, Esc goes back from the second screen; any
key stops the countdown.

flboot/flboot.conf, one setting per line, '#' starts a comment:

  timeout SECONDS            countdown; 0 starts the default at once, -1 waits
  cmdline OPTIONS...         kernel command line for the kernels found
  default TEXT               kernel version or entry title to preselect
  entry TITLE|PATH|OPTIONS   an extra entry; PATH on the ESP, \ separated

flboot starts a kernel through its EFI stub (CONFIG_EFI_STUB), passing the
command line as load options. The installed system has no initramfs, so root=
must name the root partition directly, e.g. root=PARTUUID=.... The live ISO
(build/53-iso.sh) does use one: its cmdline has initrd=\initrd.img, which the
kernel's EFI stub loads from the same volume (flboot sets the kernel's device
handle to the volume it read the kernel from), and no root=; src/flinitrd
then finds the ISO. If the firmware shows no file system on the device flboot
was loaded from (some virtual disks), flboot uses the first volume that has
flboot/flboot.conf.

To undo a broken update: start the snapshot from before it ("Start an earlier
state"), check that all is well, then choose "Make this the current state" in
the notice on the desktop, in Settings > Snapshots, or run `flsnap rollback`
(as root or a member of group seat). That swaps @ for a copy of the snapshot
(the state left becomes a snapshot itself) and sets it as the default
subvolume; restart to use it. A snapshot is read-only, so the system started
from it cannot change /; /tmp, /run and the home folders (@home) still work.

Tests: tests/menu-test.sh runs the real menu code (conf.c, menu.c, gfx.c) on a
stand-in firmware (tests/hostmenu.c) with scripted keys and writes screenshots:
  FONT=.../Lat2-Terminus16.psfu.gz tests/menu-test.sh [SHOTDIR]
